<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="031" project="spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI">
  <metadata>
    <title>Deploy to slot and run the full OBO verification checklist (FR-C1)</title>
    <phase>3 Rollout</phase>
    <gate>022 complete</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>auth tag, deployment; the gate before touching a live environment.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Prescriptive checklist execution against a running deployment.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Live deployment verification; must be operator-observed.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>022</deps>
    <tags>auth,deploy,obo,verification</tags>
    <estimated-effort>1 day</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Deploy the migrated build to the dev slot and run every item of the OBO verification checklist, plus the inbound-validation regression. Nothing flips until this is fully green.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Treat every checklist item as blocking.</role>
  <goal>A completed, recorded checklist proving every OBO-dependent surface works under MI-FIC on the slot, and that inbound token validation is unaffected.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>spec §FR-C1 and design §6.1 define the checklist. OBO fails CLOSED: a miss here means an immediate total lockout after the swap, not a degraded experience. NFR-05 adds inbound validation because AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi binds the same AzureAd config section that carries AzureAd:ClientSecret.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="canonical-reference">projects/spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI/spec.md</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/DI/AuthorizationModule.cs</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies><dependency task="022" status="pending">Migrated confidential clients</dependency></dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: SLOT ONLY. Do not swap in this task — that is 032.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: BEFORE running the checklist, verify the slot's keyVaultReferenceIdentity is the mi-bff-api-dev resource ID. Task 001 found this is a SITE property that --configuration-source does NOT copy; the slot defaulted to SystemAssigned (which the slot does not have) and every Key Vault reference failed, aborting startup with exit code 134 (SIGABRT). A slot that cannot resolve KV references fails in ways that LOOK like credential failures -- the worst possible misdiagnosis while testing a credential change. See notes/decisions/001-slot-creation.md Finding A.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: do NOT use Deploy-BffApi.ps1 -UseSlotDeploy. It deploys AND SWAPS in one flow with no -SkipSwap option (scripts/Deploy-BffApi.ps1:86-115). Task 032 owns the swap. Use the explicit az webapp deploy recipe in notes/decisions/001-slot-creation.md Finding C, or add a -SkipSwap switch to the script first.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: every checklist item is blocking. A partial pass is a FAIL; do not proceed to 032 on "mostly green".</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: verify with the MI-FIC credential FIRST in the order, and then re-verify with the secret first, proving rollback works before it is needed.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Scope: CLEAR AZURE_CLIENT_ID ON BOTH SLOTS, or record why not. Booked by task 023 (verified live 2026-08-21). AZURE_CLIENT_ID on spaarke-bff-dev holds the UAMI clientId 5967251e-..., while the app registration is 1e40baad-... . GraphClientFactory.cs:54 reads AZURE_CLIENT_ID ?? API_APP_ID as the APP-ONLY clientId, so that resolution yields a managed identity where an app registration is required. It is inert ONLY because Graph:ManagedIdentity:Enabled is true, which makes the consuming branch dead code; set that flag false -- plausible during an incident -- and the BFF builds a ClientSecretCredential from a managed identity and fails with an opaque AADSTS error naming neither identity. The single consumer falls back to API_APP_ID, which is the correct value, so clearing the setting REMOVES the trap rather than guarding it. Task 023 added a startup guard (fatal when MI is disabled, LogError when enabled) but deliberately did NOT touch live app settings -- that is this task's territory. Check the staging slot AND production. See notes/decisions/023-identity-conflation.md section 3.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>OBO verification, SPE, Office add-ins, Copilot agent</topic>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-028-spaarke-auth-architecture.md</file>
      <file>docs/guides/auth-deployment-setup.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI/notes/CREDENTIAL-INVENTORY.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="prescriptive">
    <step>Deploy the migrated build to the slot with MI-FIC first in the credential order.</step>
    <step>SPE: document upload, download and preview.</step>
    <step>AI chat: dataverse.* tool calls over /api/ai/chat SSE.</step>
    <step>Office add-ins: Outlook and Word save flows.</step>
    <step>M365 Copilot agent: /api/agent.</step>
    <step>Dataverse row-level authorization: PermissionsEndpoints.cs:56,116 and the AI authorization filters.</step>
    <step>Send-as-user email via /api/communications.</step>
    <step>Long-running OBO.</step>
    <step>Inbound validation regression: workforce JWT, the Ciam scheme, and the RagApiKey scheme all still validate.</step>
    <step>Reorder to secret-first and re-run an abbreviated pass to prove rollback.</step>
    <step>Record every result.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>ANY checklist item fails — STOP. Do not proceed to 032 and do not "fix forward" on a live path.</trigger>
    <trigger>Inbound validation changes behaviour in any way — STOP; NFR-05 exists because the AzureAd section is shared between inbound and outbound.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarke-auth-v4-dataverse-MI/notes/decisions/031-obo-verification-dev.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>Pre-check: the slot's keyVaultReferenceIdentity resolves to mi-bff-api-dev and the slot boots cleanly (no exit-134 SIGABRT), BEFORE any OBO conclusion is drawn.</criterion>
    <criterion>All eight OBO surfaces pass under MI-FIC on the slot.</criterion>
    <criterion>Inbound token validation is unchanged for all three schemes.</criterion>
    <criterion>The secret-first ordering also passes, proving rollback before it is needed.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: an unauthorised user is denied on every surface — fail-closed confirmed, never error-open.</criterion>
    <criterion>Negative case: an expired or malformed user token is rejected at the inbound layer, not at the OBO exchange.</criterion>
    <criterion>The production slot was not deployed to and not swapped.</criterion>
    <criterion>Task 030 carry-forward (MEASURED 2026-08-21): after a FIC is created or changed, Entra
      token acquisition FLAPS for roughly two minutes — 8 failures interleaved with successes over
      ~130s as replicas converge, returning AADSTS70025 (NOT 70021, which was never observed). A single
      green check within that window therefore proves nothing. Any verification here must either wait
      out the window or sample repeatedly before declaring the credential good.</criterion>
    <criterion>Task 030 carry-forward (ADR-check W-7): re-exercise task 030 acceptance criteria 3 and 4 here,
      where a real managed-identity assertion IS mintable. (3) a FIC whose subject is the UAMI's clientId is
      rejected at exchange, and (4) AADSTS70021 immediately after creation is retried, not surfaced as failure.
      Both were proven at task 030 by one-time runs — the conflation case only structurally, because a
      workstation cannot mint an assertion at all. There is no Pester harness in the repo to catch a
      regression, and PR #779 is actively rewriting the same script, so closing the loop here (where it is
      nearly free) is the only repeatable protection these two invariants get. Use
      `Register-EntraAppRegistrations.ps1 -FicOnly -AssertionToken &lt;token&gt;`.

      RESOLVED 2026-08-24 (owner decision, Option B). The premise "where a real managed-identity assertion
      IS mintable" turned out to be FALSE, measured not assumed: Kudu /api/command executes shell fine on
      the slot, but IDENTITY_ENDPOINT / IDENTITY_HEADER are ABSENT from the Kudu sidecar and no visible
      /proc/*/environ exposes them. Only the app container can mint, and the only code there is the BFF,
      which has no endpoint that emits its assertion and must never have one. Extracting an assertion to a
      workstation was refused - it is a live credential capable of authenticating as the BFF app.

      Criteria 3 and 4 are therefore recorded as VERIFIED STRUCTURALLY AT TASK 030, live platform
      confirmation not performed. This is a stated limitation with its reason, NOT a deferral to 090.
      The split that makes it acceptable: the half Spaarke can regress (does the script DETECT a
      wrong-subject FIC / DOES it retry on 70021) is proven; the half not confirmed live (Entra rejecting
      a sub/subject mismatch) is Microsoft-documented and not regressable by this codebase.

      MITIGATION, and the reason this is not left to "UAT will catch it": auth-v4 has NO remaining task
      that creates or changes a FIC (032, 033, 090 all checked), so this project's UAT would never
      exercise the path. It was handed instead to the first real consumer - customer-provisioning-
      orchestration-r1 Wave G-3 task 130 - whose first -FicOnly run against a new environment IS the live
      test, for free, in the environment that actually matters. Written up as section 11 of
      notes/PROVISIONING-CHANGE-REQUEST.md. See notes/decisions/031-obo-verification-dev.md section 6.1.
    </criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <booked-obligations>
    <!-- BOOKED BY TASK 051 (2026-08-23) — record: notes/decisions/051-sas-rotation.md -->
    <obligation source="051" id="051-A">
      The `staging` slot ALREADY EXISTS and is Running with its own app settings. This project's
      notes previously said "0 exist" — that was false and caused a ~40-minute dev outage during
      051. Do NOT assume the slot must be created. Verify its existing settings and
      keyVaultReferenceIdentity before deploying to it, and establish who created it.
    </obligation>
    <obligation source="051" id="051-B">
      Service Bus cutover. Set on BOTH SLOTS of the dev app spaarke-bff-dev (the default slot,
      which Azure calls "production", and the slot named "staging"):
        ServiceBus__FullyQualifiedNamespace = spaarke-servicebus-dev.servicebus.windows.net
      Namespace wins over the still-present SAS string, so this single setting IS the cutover and
      removing it IS the rollback (NFR-06). RBAC is already granted: mi-bff-api-dev
      (principal 9fd47efb-...) holds Azure Service Bus Data Sender + Data Receiver at NAMESPACE
      scope. Leave BOTH SAS keys in place until 033.
    </obligation>
    <obligation source="051" id="051-C">
      Verification for 051-B: /healthz must report the new `servicebus-job-processing` check
      Healthy (it reports Degraded after one auth failure, Unhealthy after three), and sdap-jobs
      must be observed draining. A 200 from /healthz alone is NOT sufficient evidence — during the
      051 outage both slots returned 200 while processing zero messages, which is exactly why that
      check now exists.
    </obligation>
    <obligation source="051" id="051-D">
      Every az command in this project must carry an explicit --subscription. The dev subscription
      is 484bc857-3802-427f-9ea5-ca47b43db0f0; the CLI default drifted to "Spaarke Model 1
      Production" during 051, which briefly made verified-correct RBAC look absent.
    </obligation>
  </booked-obligations>
</task>
